@node Compiling
@chapter Compiling

@cindex compiling
@cindex F9
@cindex compiler message format
@cindex gcc
@cindex make

xwpe integrates with external compilers through a compile-edit-debug
cycle.  The compiler is configured per language (by file extension) and
xwpe parses compiler output to jump to errors in the source code.

This chapter was verified on 2026-06-01 with xwpe 1.6.3 against GCC 15.2,
G++ 15.2, GFortran 15.2, Free Pascal 3.2.2, OpenJDK javac 25, Python 3.13,
Perl 5.40, and pdflatex (TeX Live 2025).

@section The build cycle

@cindex build cycle
@cindex Alt-C
@cindex Ctrl-F9

The build cycle uses three commands, each building on the previous:

@table @kbd
@item Alt-C (Compile)
Compile the current file only.  For GNU compilers (gcc, g++, gfortran),
xwpe invokes the compiler with @code{-c -o @var{file}.o @var{file}.c}.
For non-GNU compilers (fpc, javac), the @code{-c} and @code{-o} flags
are omitted.  Only files modified since the last compilation are
recompiled.

@item F9 or Alt-M (Make)
Recompile all modified files and link the executable.  This is the
most common command, and the bottom row of a source window names it
(@samp{F9 Build}).

@item Ctrl-F9 (Run)
Build the executable and then run it.  Program arguments can be set
via @samp{Run @result{} Arguments} (@kbd{Alt-R A}).

For interpreted languages (Python, Perl), @kbd{Ctrl-F9} runs the
script directly via the interpreter (e.g.@: @code{python3 file.py}).
In X11 mode, output is captured to the Messages buffer via
@code{popen}; no xterm is spawned.
@end table

After compilation, errors appear in the Message Window.  Use
@kbd{Alt-T} (Next Error) and @kbd{Alt-V} (Previous Error) to navigate
between errors.  The cursor jumps to the error location in the source
code, on the line and the column the compiler names.  These commands
are also available from the @samp{Run} menu as @samp{Next Error} and
@samp{Previous Error}.

@cindex Alt-F9
@cindex Alt-F8
@cindex Alt-F7
The Run menu shows only keys a terminal delivers.  Turbo C's
@kbd{Alt-F9} (Compile), @kbd{Alt-F8} (Next Error) and @kbd{Alt-F7}
(Previous Error) are kept as legacy aliases: they work where they reach
the editor (the X11 and Wayland windows, the Linux console), but
terminal emulators keep them for themselves, so the menu does not offer
them.

@subsection The build runs in the background

@cindex build, background
@cindex build, cancelling
@cindex Esc, during a build

A build does not hold the editor.  While the compiler, the linker or
@command{make} runs, every line it prints lands in Messages as it comes,
and the last row of Messages reads @samp{building ... 3s  (Esc cancels)},
counting the seconds.  The editor keeps taking keys: you can move to
another window, scroll, or read the output as it grows.

@kbd{Esc} cancels the build.  The compiler and everything it started
are stopped, Messages ends with @samp{build cancelled}, and no later
step runs: a cancelled @kbd{F9} links nothing, and a cancelled
@kbd{Ctrl-F9} runs nothing.  A second build key pressed while one runs
is refused with a line on the status row that says @kbd{Esc} cancels the
running build.

@subsection The end of a build

@cindex error count
@cindex warning count

When a build ends, the last row of Messages counts what it found, for
example @samp{1 error, 0 warnings} or @samp{0 errors, 2 warnings}.  A
build that failed without a row to walk (a linker error, a command that
could not run) adds its exit status to that row.  Each warning row starts
with a warning sign (U+26A0), so the rows that only advise stand apart
from the ones that stopped the build.  A failed build puts the cursor on
its first error; a successful one gives the keys back to the window you
were in.

@cindex nothing to build
@cindex up to date
When there is nothing to do, because the program (for @kbd{F9}) or the
object file (for @kbd{Alt-C}) is newer than its sources, Messages starts
afresh with one line, for example @samp{nothing to build: t.e is up to
date}, and the keys stay in the source window.

@subsection Walking the errors

@cindex Alt-T, order
@cindex Messages, Esc
@cindex Messages, double click

@kbd{Alt-T} and @kbd{Alt-V} visit the rows in the order the compiler
printed them, crossing from file to file: an error in an included header
that the compiler printed between two errors of the main file is visited
in its turn.  The rows of an include chain (@samp{In file included from
a.c:2:}) belong to the error after them and are never a stop of their
own; neither are the notes gcc adds under an error.

In the Messages window, @kbd{Enter} or a double click on a row jumps to
the place it names; a single click only moves the Messages cursor to the
row.  @kbd{Esc} leaves Messages for the window you came from, its cursor
where you left it.

@cindex no messages to walk
Before any build, or after one that left no row, @kbd{Alt-T} and
@kbd{Alt-V} say @samp{no messages to walk} on the status row.

@cindex file name, with blanks
@cindex long path
A row's file name is the whole name the compiler printed, blanks
included, so an error in @file{my file.c} jumps into that file, and a
path of any length makes a row.  When a pattern lets the name take a
lead-in with it (Python's @samp{File "t.py"}, a Scala 3 heading, make's
@samp{[Makefile}), the longest part after a blank or a bracket that
names a real file is used.  A relative name is looked for in the editor's
own directory and then in the directory the build ran in, so @kbd{Alt-T}
to the second file of a make started from another directory opens it.  A
row naming a file that exists in neither place says @samp{cannot find
@var{name}} on the status row; no window is opened and nothing is written
to disk.

@subsection Compared with Turbo C

@cindex Turbo C, compile box

Turbo C held the whole IDE while it compiled, behind a box that counted
lines, warnings and errors until the compiler was done.  xwpe keeps the
editor live and shows the waiting line in Messages instead, and @kbd{Esc}
cancels the build, because a build of a large project runs for minutes
and nothing about it needs the editor to wait.

@subsection Header dependencies

@cindex header dependencies
@cindex recompilation
@cindex check headers

xwpe recompiles a source file not only when the file itself changes, but
also when any header it @code{#include}s has changed since the last
build.  It finds those headers by scanning the source for @code{#include
"@var{file}"} directives (recursively, through the headers too).

Crucially, it ignores @code{#include}s that are not actually in effect:
those inside a @code{//} or @code{/* @dots{} */} comment, and those
inside a @code{#if 0 @dots{} #endif} block.  A commented-out or disabled
include is not a real dependency, so editing such a header does not force
a needless recompile.

The example @file{docs/examples/headers_test.c} demonstrates this: it
includes the real header @file{headers_test.h}, mentions @file{fake.h}
inside a @code{//} comment, and includes @file{disabled.h} inside an
@code{#if 0} block.  Editing @file{headers_test.h} and pressing @key{F9}
recompiles; editing @file{fake.h} or @file{disabled.h} does not.

@section Configuring the compiler

@cindex compiler configuration
@cindex Options menu

The compiler is configured via @samp{Options @result{} Compiler}
(@kbd{Alt-O C}).  Each language is identified by its file extension
(e.g.@: @file{.c} for C, @file{.cpp} for C++).

Settings per language:
@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Compiler}: path or name of the compiler (e.g.@: @code{gcc}).
@item @strong{Compiler Options}: flags passed to the compiler
(e.g.@: @code{-Wall -g}).
@item @strong{Loader Options}: flags passed to the linker
(e.g.@: @code{-lm}).
@item @strong{Executable Name}: name of the output binary
(default: derived from the source file name).
@item @strong{Compiler Style}: @code{GNU} or @code{Other}.
@code{GNU} uses the built-in @code{file:line:column:} pattern.
@code{Other} uses a custom interpreter string that you define.
@end itemize

@section Language support

@cindex language support

xwpe selects the compiler automatically based on the file extension.
The table lists the default entries; Go and Rust have entries too (see
the note below).

@cindex no compiler entry
@kbd{F9} on a file whose extension has no entry says, for example,
@samp{no compiler entry for .xyz; add one in Options > Compiler}.

@subsection Default compilers (v1.6.3)

@multitable @columnfractions .12 .14 .22 .22 .30
@headitem Language @tab Compiler @tab Extensions @tab Error format @tab Notes
@item C @tab gcc @tab .c @tab @code{file:line:col:} @tab
  GNU style.  Cursor jumps to exact column.
@item C++ @tab g++ @tab .cpp .cc .C .cxx @tab @code{file:line:col:} @tab
  Same error format as gcc.
@item Fortran @tab gfortran @tab .f .f90 .f95 .f03 .f08 @tab @code{file:line:col:} @tab
  GNU style.  Blank lines in output are handled.
@item Pascal @tab fpc @tab .p .pas .pp @tab @code{file(line,col)} @tab
  Dedicated pattern.  Cursor jumps to exact column.
@item Java @tab javac @tab .java @tab @code{file:line:} @tab
  No column in javac output.  Cursor goes to start of line.
@item Python @tab python3 @tab .py @tab @code{File file, line N} @tab
  Uses @code{-m py_compile}.  Ctrl-F9 runs the script.
@item LaTeX @tab pdflatex @tab .tex @tab @code{file:line:} @tab
  Uses @code{-interaction=nonstopmode -file-line-error}.
@item Perl @tab perl @tab .pl .pm @tab @code{at file line N} @tab
  Uses @code{-c} for syntax check.  Ctrl-F9 runs the script.
@item COBOL @tab cobc @tab .cob .cbl @tab @code{file:line:} @tab
  GNU COBOL.  GNU style with @code{-g}.
@item Algol 68 @tab ga68 / a68g @tab .a68 .alg @tab (by toolchain) @tab
  Two source-incompatible toolchains; xwpe picks one per file from its
  dialect (see below).  ga68 (GCC front-end) compiles a native binary;
  a68g (Algol 68 Genie) interprets, with @code{--norun} for the F9 check.
@item Scala @tab scalac @tab .scala @tab @code{file:line:} @tab
  Uses @code{-deprecation}.  scalac writes the class files itself, so
  there is no link step.  Scala 3's @samp{-- [E007] @dots{}: t.scala:3:17}
  heading makes a row too.
@end multitable

@subsection Other GNU-compatible compilers

Any compiler that produces errors in the @code{file:line:column: message}
format works with xwpe's default @code{GNU} compiler style without
configuration.  Known compatible compilers include:

@itemize @bullet
@item clang, clang++ (LLVM C/C++)
@item rustc (Rust)
@item go build (Go)
@item dmd, ldc2 (D)
@item valac (Vala)
@item ghc (Haskell)
@item zig (Zig)
@item nim (Nim)
@item GNU Guile (Scheme)
@end itemize

To use one of these, go to @samp{Options @result{} Compiler}, set the
compiler name and file extension, and select @code{GNU} style.

@quotation Note
Go (@code{.go}), Rust (@code{.rs}) and Scala (@code{.scala}) also have
@emph{source-level debugging} over the Debug Adapter Protocol, where the
build is driven by the debug toolchain itself (Delve builds the Go package,
xwpe compiles Rust with @code{rustc -g}, and Bloop builds the Scala target).
See @ref{Debugging}.  All three are syntax-highlighted -- see
@ref{Configuration} (@file{syntax_def}).
@end quotation

@section The interpreter string (pattern language)
@anchor{Interpreter string}

@cindex interpreter string
@cindex pattern language
@cindex error parsing
@cindex message-string

When @code{Compiler Style} is set to @code{Other}, xwpe uses a custom
@dfn{interpreter string} to parse compiler output.  This is a pattern
that tells xwpe how to extract the filename, line number, and column
from each error message.

@subsection Pattern syntax

The interpreter string is a mix of literal text and special placeholders.
xwpe matches each line of compiler output against this pattern.

@subsubsection Wildcards

@multitable @columnfractions .15 .85
@headitem Pattern @tab Meaning
@item @code{*} @tab Match any sequence of characters (including empty).
@item @code{?} @tab Match any single character.
@item @code{[abc]} @tab Match any one of the characters listed.
@item @code{[!abc]} @tab Match any character NOT listed.
@item @code{[a-z]} @tab Match any character in the range.
@end multitable

@subsubsection Variable placeholders

These capture parts of the error message:

@multitable @columnfractions .40 .60
@headitem Placeholder @tab Meaning
@item @code{$@{FILE@}} @tab Filename containing the error.
@item @code{$@{LINE@}} @tab Line number of the error (must be a digit).
@item @code{$@{LINE+N@}} or @code{$@{LINE-N@}} @tab Line number with
  offset adjustment.
@item @code{$@{COLUMN@}} @tab Column number (numeric), counted from 1
  as compilers print it.
@item @code{$@{COLUMN+N@}} or @code{$@{COLUMN-N@}} @tab Column with
  offset.
@item @code{$@{COLUMN=BEFORE@}} @tab Column is determined by searching
  for a string that appears BEFORE the error position.  The captured
  text is searched in the source line.
@item @code{$@{COLUMN=AFTER@}} @tab Column is determined by searching
  for a string that appears AFTER the error position.
@item @code{$@{CMPTEXT@}} @tab Captures the compiler's error text
  (used with multi-line formats).
@end multitable

@subsubsection Warning detection

@multitable @columnfractions .40 .60
@headitem Placeholder @tab Meaning
@item @code{$@{?*:STRING@}} @tab If @code{STRING} appears anywhere in
  the line, treat this as a warning rather than an error.  A warning
  row is counted as a warning and marked with the warning sign; a
  build with warnings only succeeds.  Alt-T/Alt-V visit warning rows
  as they visit errors.
@item @code{$@{?N:STRING@}} @tab Like the above, but @code{STRING}
  must appear at column @var{N} (0-based).
@end multitable

@subsubsection Multi-line patterns

Patterns can span multiple lines of compiler output by using @code{\n}
as a line separator.  Up to 4 lines can be matched.  The first line
must match the filename; subsequent lines can match additional details
like line numbers, column indicators, or error text.

@subsection Built-in patterns

xwpe ships with three built-in pattern strings:

@table @code
@item GNU (default)
@code{$@{?*:warning:@}$@{FILE@}:$@{LINE@}:$@{COLUMN@}:*}

Matches: @code{hello.c:3:29: error: expected ';' before 'ret'}

This pattern is used automatically when Compiler Style is @code{GNU}.
It works with gcc, g++, gfortran, clang, rustc, Go, and any compiler
that uses the @code{file:line:column:} format.

@item cc (classic UNIX cc)
@code{$@{?*:warning:@}"$@{FILE@}", line $@{LINE@}:* at or near * "$@{COLUMN=AFTER@}"}

Matches: @code{"hello.c", line 3: error at or near "foo"}

@item Pascal (Turbo Pascal / old FPC)
Multi-line pattern for the classic Turbo Pascal error format.  Modern
Free Pascal (fpc) uses a different format; see below.
@end table

@subsection Examples

@subsubsection Free Pascal (fpc)

FPC errors look like:

@example
hello.pas(6,10) Error: Incompatible types
hello.pas(7,10) Error: Incompatible types
@end example

Pattern (configured by default in xwpe 1.6.2):

@example
$@{?*:Warning:@}$@{FILE@}($@{LINE@},$@{COLUMN@})*
@end example

Breakdown:
@itemize @bullet
@item @code{$@{?*:Warning:@}} -- if ``Warning:'' appears, treat as
  warning, not error.
@item @code{$@{FILE@}(} -- capture everything before @code{(} as the
  filename.
@item @code{$@{LINE@},} -- capture digits before @code{,} as line
  number.
@item @code{$@{COLUMN@})} -- capture digits before @code{)} as column.
@item @code{*} -- match the rest of the line (the error message).
@end itemize

@subsubsection javac (Java)

javac errors look like:

@example
Hello.java:3: error: incompatible types
        int x = "hello";
                ^
@end example

Pattern (configured by default in xwpe 1.6.2):

@example
$@{?*:warning:@}$@{FILE@}:$@{LINE@}:*
@end example

Note: javac does not report a column number, so the cursor goes to
the beginning of the error line.

@subsubsection Python (python3 -m py_compile)

Python compile errors look like:

@example
  File "/path/to/hello.py", line 3
    x = (1 +
        ^
SyntaxError: '(' was never closed
@end example

Pattern (configured by default):

@example
*$@{FILE@}, line $@{LINE@}
@end example

@noindent
@kbd{Ctrl-F9} runs the Python script directly via @code{python3}
and captures output in the Messages buffer.

@subsubsection Perl (perl -c)

Perl syntax check errors look like:

@example
syntax error at hello.pl line 6, near "print"
@end example

Pattern (configured by default):

@example
*at $@{FILE@} line $@{LINE@}
@end example

@noindent
@kbd{Ctrl-F9} runs the Perl script directly via @code{perl}
and captures output in the Messages buffer.

@subsubsection gfortran (Fortran)

gfortran errors look like:

@example
hello.f90:3:6:

    3 |   a = "x"
      |      1
Error: Cannot convert CHARACTER(1) to INTEGER(4) at (1)
@end example

@noindent
gfortran uses the GNU @code{file:line:column:} format, so the built-in
GNU pattern applies and the cursor jumps to the exact column.

@subsubsection cobc (COBOL)

GnuCOBOL errors look like:

@example
hello.cob:6: error: 'X' is not defined
@end example

Pattern (configured by default):

@example
$@{FILE@}:$@{LINE@}:*
@end example

@noindent
GNU COBOL is GNU-style; xwpe compiles with @code{cobc -x -g}.

@subsubsection pdflatex (LaTeX)

With @code{-file-line-error}, pdflatex errors look like:

@example
./hello.tex:3: Undefined control sequence.
@end example

Pattern (configured by default):

@example
$@{FILE@}:$@{LINE@}:*
@end example

@noindent
xwpe runs @code{pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode -file-line-error} so
the @code{file:line:} prefix the pattern needs is always present.

@subsubsection Algol 68: two dialects (ga68 and a68g)

@cindex Algol 68
@cindex ga68
@cindex a68g
@cindex stropping

Algol 68 has two free toolchains, and they are @emph{source
incompatible} -- a program written for one will not even parse with the
other, because they use opposite @dfn{stropping} regimes (how reserved
"bold" words and comments are written):

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{ga68} -- the GNU Algol 68 front-end (part of GCC).  A native
compiler: @key{F9} Make builds and links a real executable, which
@kbd{Ctrl-F9} runs and gdb debugs.  Uses the @emph{modern} regime.
@item
@strong{a68g} -- Algol 68 Genie, an interpreter with a built-in monitor.
@key{F9} Make runs @code{a68g --norun} as a syntax check; @kbd{Ctrl-F9}
interprets the program.  Uses the @emph{classic} regime.
@end itemize

You do not choose between them: xwpe reads each @file{.a68}/@file{.alg}
file's first lines, detects its dialect, and drives the matching
toolchain for both compiling and debugging.  If only one of the two is
installed, that one is always used.

@noindent
The same ``Hello'' program in each dialect (both ship as samples under
@file{tests/inputs/}):

@need 800
@noindent
@strong{Modern (ga68)} -- lowercase bold words, @code{@{ @}} comments:

@verbatim
{ hello_ga68.a68 -- GNU Algol 68 (ga68), modern stropping. }
begin
   puts ("Hello, GNU Algol 68!'n")
end
@end verbatim

@need 800
@noindent
@strong{Classic (a68g)} -- UPPER bold words, @code{#@dots{}#} comments:

@verbatim
BEGIN
   # hello.a68 -- Algol 68 Genie (a68g), classic stropping. #
   print(("Hello, Algol 68!", new line))
END
@end verbatim

@noindent
At a glance:

@multitable @columnfractions .24 .38 .38
@headitem @tab ga68 (modern) @tab a68g (classic)
@item Bold words @tab lowercase @code{begin}/@code{end}/@code{int}
  @tab UPPER @code{BEGIN}/@code{END}/@code{INT}
@item Comments @tab @code{@{ @dots{} @}} @tab @code{# @dots{} #} or @code{CO @dots{} CO}
@item Print a line @tab @code{puts ("@dots{}'n")} @tab @code{print ((@dots{}, new line))}
@item Toolchain @tab GCC Algol 68 front-end @tab Algol 68 Genie interpreter
@item Make (@key{F9}) @tab compiles + links a binary @tab @code{a68g --norun} (check only)
@item Debug @tab gdb on the binary; breaks at @code{__algol68_main}
  @tab @code{a68g --monitor} on the source
@item Diagnostics @tab GCC @code{file:line:column:} @tab the source line, numbered (no file name)
@end multitable

@subsubsection a68g error format

Algol 68 Genie 3 reports type and syntax errors like:

@example
3       print((x, yy))
                  1
a68g: error: 1: tag "yy" has not been declared properly, in
collateral-clause starting at "(" in this line.
@end example

Pattern (configured by default):

@example
$@{LINE@} *
@end example

@noindent
The row is the echoed source line, which starts with its line number;
@kbd{Enter} on it, or on the message under it, jumps to that line.
a68g names no file: a pattern without @code{$@{FILE@}} names the file
being compiled.  A line that starts with @samp{a68g:} is the compiler's
own message, not a linker's, so the rows above it are still walked.

@subsubsection ga68 error format

ga68 is a GCC front-end, so it emits the standard driver diagnostics
with a @code{file:line:column:} prefix -- the same format as gcc/g++ --
and the error jump lands exactly on the offending token.  Note that
ga68's user entry point is @code{__algol68_main}, not @code{main}; this
matters when debugging (@pxref{Debugging}).

@subsubsection Adding a new compiler

To add support for a compiler not in the default list:

@enumerate
@item
Open @samp{Options @result{} Compiler} (@kbd{Alt-O C}).
@item
Add the language (e.g.@: ``Rust'') with its file extension
(e.g.@: @file{.rs}).
@item
Set the compiler name (e.g.@: @code{rustc}).
@item
If the compiler uses @code{file:line:column:} format, select
@code{GNU} style.  You are done.
@item
If the compiler uses a different format, select @code{Other} and
enter an interpreter string matching the format.  Run the compiler
manually first to see its error output, then construct the pattern.
@end enumerate

@subsubsection Debugging a pattern

If error navigation does not work (Alt-T/Alt-V do nothing after F9):

@enumerate
@item
Check the Messages window -- are compiler errors visible?
@item
Run the compiler manually in a terminal and compare its output
format against your pattern.
@item
Verify the pattern captures @code{$@{FILE@}} and @code{$@{LINE@}}
at minimum.
@item
If the compiler emits blank lines between errors, verify you are
running xwpe 1.6.2 or later (earlier versions truncated output at
blank lines).
@end enumerate

@section Known limitations

@cindex compiler limitations

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{GNU-style flags}: For @code{GNU} style compilers, xwpe
  automatically adds @code{-c} and @code{-o @var{file}.o} to the
  compile command.  For @code{Other} style compilers, these flags are
  omitted.  If your compiler is GNU-compatible but needs @code{Other}
  style for its error format, add @code{-c} to the Compiler Options
  field manually.
@item @strong{javac column}: javac does not report column numbers in
  its primary error line.  The cursor goes to the start of the error
  line, not the exact position.
@item @strong{Multi-file errors}: error navigation assumes each error
  references a single file.  If the error file is not currently open,
  xwpe opens it automatically; if it does not exist, the status row says
  @samp{cannot find @var{name}}.
@end itemize

